&lt;p&gt;The Donald Trump administration will take 60 days to assess the potential global economic impact of imposing sweeping new sanctions on Russia and its trading partners, including India, US Secretary of State Marco Rubio said on Saturday.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Explaining Washington's decision to waive sanctions under the Lindsey Graham Act this week, Rubio pointed to mounting pressure on global energy prices amid the war in Ukraine, the conflict involving Iran and Houthi attacks on Saudi Arabia.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rubio made the remarks while speaking to reporters ahead of his visit to Uzbekistan and India, highlighting the need to weigh the economic consequences of fresh restrictions against the broader geopolitical situation.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Why Trump Is Reviewing Russia Sanctions&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rubio said the legislation included provisions for waivers to give the administration time to assess the implications of its measures, particularly amid disruptions to global energy supplies.&lt;/p&gt;
